WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.506
Hey there, CFP candidates.

2
00:00:01.506 --> 00:00:09.325
Welcome to this high yield explainer covering the exact traps and highly tested rules you need to know for group and employee benefits.

3
00:00:09.325 --> 00:00:11.294
You've already studied the basics.

4
00:00:11.294 --> 00:00:13.264
You know what health insurance is.

5
00:00:13.264 --> 00:00:15.928
But today, we aren't just reading definitions.

6
00:00:15.928 --> 00:00:21.141
We are actively hunting for the specific scenarios where the test makers try to trick you.

7
00:00:21.141 --> 00:00:27.686
We're going to break down the taxation rules and the structural traps that separate passing scores from the rest.

8
00:00:27.686 --> 00:00:28.728
Let's get into it.

9
00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:35.447
Here's what we're tackling today: Section 79, group term life and imputed income math example, section 125 and FSAs,

10
00:00:35.447 --> 00:00:39.617
the non-discrimination rule trap, and finally, our top five exam points.

11
00:00:39.617 --> 00:00:43.903
Okay, let's kick things off with section one, section 79, group term life.

12
00:00:43.903 --> 00:00:49.000
So, section 79 allows employers to offer a group term life insurance to their employees.

13
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.908
It's an awesome deal for the employer because they get to deduct those premiums as a business expense.

14
00:00:54.908 --> 00:00:59.657
But for your exam, the test makers don't really care about the employer side here.

15
00:00:59.657 --> 00:01:03.769
They are laser focused on how these premiums are taxed to the employee.

16
00:01:03.769 --> 00:01:10.546
They want to see if you know exactly when this tax-free perk suddenly crosses the line and turns into taxable income.

17
00:01:10.546 --> 00:01:13.326
And that brings us to the magic number, $50,000.

18
00:01:13.326 --> 00:01:16.570
You absolutely need to burn this number into your brain.

19
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:16.801
Why?

20
00:01:16.801 --> 00:01:22.883
Because the first $50,000 of employer provided group term life coverage is 100% tax-free to the employee.

21
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:25.895
The exam is going to try to trick you here for sure.

22
00:01:25.895 --> 00:01:32.208
They'll throw a massive policy amount at you and try to bait you into calculating the tax on the whole thing.

23
00:01:32.208 --> 00:01:33.251
Don't fall for it.

24
00:01:33.251 --> 00:01:38.174
Only the cost of coverage that goes over $50,000 creates what we call imputed income.

25
00:01:38.174 --> 00:01:43.271
And that excess is what gets fully taxed to the employee based on the IRS Table I rates.

26
00:01:43.271 --> 00:01:48.194
Moving right along to section two, let's walk through an imputed income math example.

27
00:01:48.194 --> 00:01:54.855
Because look, knowing the rule is great, but actually calculating that imputed income on the coverage over 50, 000,

28
00:01:54.855 --> 00:01:58.156
that is exactly where points are won or lost on test day.

29
00:01:58.156 --> 00:02:02.210
Let's look at a classic scenario with an employee named John Alvarado.

30
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:06.902
John is 52 years old and his company gives him a $250,000 group term life policy.

31
00:02:06.902 --> 00:02:10.493
Now, the trap is immediately trying to tax that full $250,000.

32
00:02:10.493 --> 00:02:11.999
But we know better, right?

33
00:02:11.999 --> 00:02:15.648
We're going to instantly slice off that first tax-free $50,000.

34
00:02:15.648 --> 00:02:18.544
That leaves John with $200,000 of excess coverage.

35
00:02:18.544 --> 00:02:26.421
The IRS taxes this excess using a standardized rate called Table I, which essentially gives you a cost per $1,000 of coverage per month.

36
00:02:26.421 --> 00:02:29.143
And for a 52-year-old, that Table I rate is 23.

37
00:02:29.143 --> 00:02:33.545
So, we'll be calculating the tax on that $200,000 excess using that 23 rate.

38
00:02:33.545 --> 00:02:39.221
You're going to want to memorize these exact four steps to quickly find the annual imputed income.

39
00:02:39.221 --> 00:02:43.739
Step one, take the total face amount, 250,000, and subtract that magic 50,000.

40
00:02:43.739 --> 00:02:44.607
We get 200,000.

41
00:02:44.607 --> 00:02:49.589
Step two, divide that excess by 1,000 simply because the IRS rate is priced per 1,000.

42
00:02:49.589 --> 00:02:50.631
That gives us 200.

43
00:02:50.631 --> 00:02:54.222
Step three, multiply that 200 by our given Table I rate of 23.

44
00:02:54.222 --> 00:02:57.524
And finally, step four, multiply by 12 for the full year.

45
00:02:57.524 --> 00:02:58.508
The result, $552.

46
00:02:58.508 --> 00:03:02.563
That $552 is the imputed income John actually has to report on his W2.

47
00:03:02.563 --> 00:03:09.397
Seriously, if you just stick to these four steps, you will never fall for the trap of taxing the entire death benefit.

48
00:03:09.397 --> 00:03:12.119
All right, section three, section 125 and FSAs.

49
00:03:12.119 --> 00:03:17.795
Now that we've completely crushed life insurance, let's pivot over to section 125 cafeteria plans.

50
00:03:17.795 --> 00:03:23.761
Basically, these plans let employees choose between taking taxable cash or opting for pre-tax benefits.

51
00:03:23.761 --> 00:03:26.541
Obviously, choosing the benefits saves on taxes.

52
00:03:26.541 --> 00:03:35.171
And one of the most heavily tested and honestly most easily confused options under this cafeteria plan umbrella is the flexible spending arrangement,

53
00:03:35.171 --> 00:03:35.577
or FSA.

54
00:03:35.577 --> 00:03:41.600
Let's look a little closer at the FSA rules because there are some really tricky exam traps hiding here.

55
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.307
First up, FSAs are funded by pre-tax employee salary reductions.

56
00:03:45.307 --> 00:03:46.292
And this is huge.

57
00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:50.868
Not only does this save on income tax, but it also saves on FICA payroll taxes.

58
00:03:50.868 --> 00:03:52.142
That's an immediate 7.

59
00:03:52.142 --> 00:03:54.343
65% tax savings right out of the gate.

60
00:03:54.343 --> 00:03:58.687
For 2025, the maximum deferral for unreimbursed medical expenses is $3,300.

61
00:03:58.687 --> 00:04:03.262
Now, as you probably know, FSAs are notorious for their use it or lose it rule.

62
00:04:03.262 --> 00:04:07.433
If you have money left over at the end of the year, it's generally gone.

63
00:04:07.433 --> 00:04:12.819
The IRS does throw us a bone with a couple of exceptions, but it's a strict either-or choice.

64
00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:19.596
An employer can offer a 2 and a half month grace period, or they can allow up to a $660 carryover into the next year.

65
00:04:19.596 --> 00:04:21.681
They can literally never offer both.

66
00:04:21.681 --> 00:04:25.619
Now, here's a fascinating trap that is highly tested: employer risk.

67
00:04:25.619 --> 00:04:31.469
Let's say an employee Walter pledges 3,300 for the year, which is deducted evenly from his paychecks.

68
00:04:31.469 --> 00:04:38.477
By February, he's only paid in $550 bucks, but he has a major medical emergency and incurs $1,500 in out-of-pocket costs.

69
00:04:38.477 --> 00:04:42.416
The plan actually has to reimburse him that full $1,500 immediately.

70
00:04:42.416 --> 00:04:47.281
And what if Walter just quits his job the very next day before paying the rest back?

71
00:04:47.281 --> 00:04:49.540
Well, the employer has to eat the loss.

72
00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:53.768
That shortfall comes straight out of the employer's pocket, not Walter's.

73
00:04:53.768 --> 00:04:55.216
And here's another thing.

74
00:04:55.216 --> 00:05:00.081
The exam absolutely loves to test the differences between similar sounding accounts.

75
00:05:00.081 --> 00:05:04.541
So, whatever you do, never treat an FSA like a health savings account or HSA.

76
00:05:04.541 --> 00:05:08.132
Remember, unlike an HSA, an FSA is strictly use it or lose it.

77
00:05:08.132 --> 00:05:09.812
Also, an FSA is not portable.

78
00:05:09.812 --> 00:05:12.824
If you leave your job, you leave the account behind.

79
00:05:12.824 --> 00:05:16.183
And you definitely cannot invest FSA funds for retirement.

80
00:05:16.183 --> 00:05:19.195
On the flip side, an HSA rolls over year after year.

81
00:05:19.195 --> 00:05:24.929
It's totally portable when you switch jobs, and you can actually invest those funds for the future.

82
00:05:24.929 --> 00:05:31.474
So, if a test question is describing a highly portable investable account, you are looking at an HSA, not an FSA.

83
00:05:31.474 --> 00:05:35.470
Okay, let's head into section four, the non-discrimination rule trap.

84
00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:42.073
Let's build on what we've learned and look at an overarching behavioral rule, the non-discrimination requirements.

85
00:05:42.073 --> 00:05:44.332
The tax code is super clear about this.

86
00:05:44.332 --> 00:05:49.023
Employee benefits are designed to benefit everybody, not just the top executives.

87
00:05:49.023 --> 00:05:56.785
If an employer's self-insured health plan, life insurance plan, or cafeteria plan violates these rules and unfairly favors the bosses,

88
00:05:56.785 --> 00:05:58.059
the penalty is severe.

89
00:05:58.059 --> 00:06:01.592
The benefit becomes completely taxable for the favored group.

90
00:06:01.592 --> 00:06:05.936
So the absolutely crucial point here is knowing exactly who pays the price.

91
00:06:05.936 --> 00:06:10.338
The exam will try to trip you up by saying that if a plan is discriminatory,

92
00:06:10.338 --> 00:06:14.103
the entire plan gets disqualified and everybody has to pay taxes.

93
00:06:14.103 --> 00:06:15.782
But that is completely false.

94
00:06:15.782 --> 00:06:20.184
Only the highly compensated employees or key employees lose their tax break.

95
00:06:20.184 --> 00:06:25.049
For example, in a discriminatory group life plan, those key employees lose that $50,

96
00:06:25.049 --> 00:06:31.073
000 exclusion entirely and they get taxed on the greater of the actual premium cost or the Table I cost.

97
00:06:31.073 --> 00:06:34.027
But what about the rank and file non-key employees?

98
00:06:34.027 --> 00:06:36.691
They are completely unaffected by the penalty.

99
00:06:36.691 --> 00:06:39.298
They still get their first 50 grand tax-free.

100
00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:44.684
Plus, the employer can generally still deduct the premiums paid as a normal business expense.

101
00:06:44.684 --> 00:06:48.565
The punishment falls directly and solely on the favored executives.

102
00:06:48.565 --> 00:06:52.445
All right, we've made it to section five, our top five exam points.

103
00:06:52.445 --> 00:06:58.006
We're in the final stretch, so let's lock in everything we've covered with a rapid-fire summary.

104
00:06:58.006 --> 00:07:00.960
Keep this hit list fresh in your mind for test day.

105
00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:04.435
Number one, never ever tax the first 50k of group term life.

106
00:07:04.435 --> 00:07:05.072
Number two,

107
00:07:05.072 --> 00:07:13.065
know exactly how to run that four-step Table I math formula and remember to only calculate imputed income on the coverage amount over 50K.

108
00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:16.945
Number three, fiercely separate your FSA rules from your HSA rules.

109
00:07:16.945 --> 00:07:18.509
They're not the same thing.

110
00:07:18.509 --> 00:07:21.058
Number four, memorize the FSA safety valves.

111
00:07:21.058 --> 00:07:22.274
A plan can offer a 2.

112
00:07:22.274 --> 00:07:25.923
5-month grace period or up to a $660 carryover, but never both.

113
00:07:25.923 --> 00:07:31.020
And finally, number five, remember who actually pays the price in a discriminatory plan.

114
00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:36.233
It's the highly compensated and key employees, the rank and file remain totally untouched.

115
00:07:36.233 --> 00:07:42.140
With these rules and traps mapped out, the math clarified, and the structural differences highlighted,

116
00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:45.731
you are totally ready to conquer the employee benefit section.

117
00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:52.276
But ask yourself this, if the IRS makes the rules this complex just for basic life insurance and health accounts,

118
00:07:52.276 --> 00:07:56.794
what kinds of traps are waiting for you in retirement planning and estate tax?

119
00:07:56.794 --> 00:07:59.922
The exam never stops testing your attention to detail.

120
00:07:59.922 --> 00:08:04.787
Keep digging, trust your preparation, and thanks so much for joining this explainer.

